.THS 8Bit 3L News.SH NAME$N - A small Library to manage language dependent character with 8th bitsupport. .SH FUNCTIONSvoid.B setup_convtable(doit, rev).brint doit;.brint rev;void.B conv_charset(buf, siz).brchar *buf;.brint siz;char.B conv_char(c).brchar c;.SH DESCRIPTION.ti +3This library allows software to handle different standards ofinternational character sets. As a suggestion all characters should betransformed to a unique standard (ISO 8859/1). The MNews system convertsthe local written character to the ISO character set and the Newsreadercan convert it back if desired..PPTo use the library you have to call first.B setup_convtable()with two arguments. The first tells the library if converting shouldtake place and the second one if the converting should be done to orfrom ISO (e.g. MNews uses to ISO, so the 2nd parameter is FALSE, theNewsreader converts back from ISO, so the parameter is TRUE.) To converta block of characters use.B conv_charset()with two arguments. The first is a pointer to a buffer where the data isplaced (and will be stored back) and the second is the number of bytesto convert. To convert a single character use.B conv_char()with the source character as an argument; the matching character will bereturned. .PPThe library determinates the type of the used character set by examninthe enviroment variable.B CHARSETto examine then a file where the converting table is stored. The filehas the name of the value of the enviroment variable and is located in.B /h0/USR/LIB/CHARSETor can be specified with the entry.B CHARSET.LIBin the Sysinfo database..SH FORMAT OF A CONVERTING TABLE.ti +3Each line in this file is a converting rule for one character. A linewith a leading hash (`#') is treated as a comment. The format of a lineis.ce<local-char>     <iso-char>where one can use several versions of expressing these characters. Thetype of expression is determinated using the prefix. Following prefixesare allowed:.O 'the following character is the used literal..NO 1..9The number is treated as a decimal number..NO 0xthe following number is treated as a hexadecimal number in the range ofa byte (0 - 255)..NO 0the number is teated as an octal number..NO %the number is treated as a binary number..EO.PPFor hexadecimal there may also be a dollar sign (`\$') instead of the `0x'and for decimal numbers there may be a hash sign (`#') to mark them.NOTE: never use the hash sign at the beginning of a line; this line willbe treated as a comment..SH EXAMPLE OF A FILE.raw 3##  Convert IBM to ISO characterset#  (only german Umlaut)#\$8e    \$c4\$99    \$d6\$9a    \$dc\$84    \$e4\$94    \$f6\$81    \$fc\$e1    \$df.noraw